WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.250
Think about how you handle an
unexpected request in everyday life.

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:06.130
You might ask who is asking,

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:09.503
why they need something,
and how you can check the story.

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:12.010
Those habits are valuable online too.

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:13.240
In this lesson,

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:15.810
we will examine a
fictional urgent message,

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:20.430
compare different protections for a file,
and choose permissions that fit a task.

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:24.110
You do not need to identify
every kind of attack by name.

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:26.390
You need a repeatable decision process:

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:28.610
pause, inspect the evidence,

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:32.410
verify independently,
and choose an action that limits risk.

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:36.470
Use fictional examples throughout.
There is no reason to open

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:37.510
a suspicious link

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:40.256
or expose a private account
to complete the practice.

00:00:42.217 --> 00:00:45.477
A workable setup supports
attention as well as visibility.

00:00:46.097 --> 00:00:46.957
Adjust the chair,

00:00:47.257 --> 00:00:48.008
screen position,

00:00:48.497 --> 00:00:49.247
text size,

00:00:49.557 --> 00:00:53.877
and sound to suit the task and your
comfort, following the lab's arrangements.

00:00:54.617 --> 00:00:56.777
Take a screen-free break when needed.

00:00:57.657 --> 00:00:59.730
People differ in what they find helpful;

00:00:59.937 --> 00:01:02.917
do not assume one text size
or pace suits everyone.

00:01:03.657 --> 00:01:04.857
Captions, clear labels,

00:01:05.337 --> 00:01:09.817
keyboard access, and instructions that
do not rely on color can help many

00:01:09.817 --> 00:01:14.657
learners. These are examples of designing
for a wider range of users rather than

00:01:14.657 --> 00:01:15.417
treating access

00:01:15.417 --> 00:01:16.617
as an afterthought.

00:01:18.457 --> 00:01:22.817
Pause and choose one control in this
course that makes the work easier for you.

00:01:23.317 --> 00:01:25.493
Explain the benefit in terms of your task.

00:01:27.281 --> 00:01:28.941
Our fictional message says

00:01:28.941 --> 00:01:32.621
your service will stop unless you
act immediately through a link.

00:01:33.361 --> 00:01:37.441
The urgency is a reason to slow down,
not proof that the request is genuine.

00:01:38.281 --> 00:01:42.322
Look at what the sender wants you to do:
reveal a password, provide a payment,

00:01:42.661 --> 00:01:45.221
open an attachment,
or follow an unfamiliar link.

00:01:46.041 --> 00:01:48.361
A convincing name or logo can be copied.

00:01:49.301 --> 00:01:52.561
Even a message from a familiar
account deserves a check when

00:01:52.561 --> 00:01:53.661
the request is unusual.

00:01:54.681 --> 00:01:57.272
Avoid trying to prove
safety by clicking the link.

00:01:57.601 --> 00:02:00.261
Instead,
describe the claim in plain language.

00:02:01.021 --> 00:02:03.881
Someone says I must provide
information immediately.

00:02:04.761 --> 00:02:07.611
That helps you separate the
pressure from the evidence.

00:02:09.303 --> 00:02:11.843 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Open the organization's
known website yourself,

00:02:12.423 --> 00:02:16.257 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
use a saved bookmark you trust,
or find its contact details independently.

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:21.089 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Do not use the suspicious message's
phone number as your independent check.

00:02:21.623 --> 00:02:25.723 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Ask whether the claimed issue exists
and what action, if any, is needed.

00:02:26.403 --> 00:02:27.723 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
If you already acted, stop

00:02:27.723 --> 00:02:28.543 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
further interaction

00:02:28.543 --> 00:02:29.963 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
and contact the appropriate

00:02:29.963 --> 00:02:31.725 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
official support channel promptly;

00:02:32.323 --> 00:02:34.706 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
in the lab,
tell the instructor what happened.

00:02:34.843 --> 00:02:37.485 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
The exact recovery steps
depend on what was shared.

00:02:37.883 --> 00:02:39.043 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Pause and choose between

00:02:39.043 --> 00:02:42.929 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
replying to the message for reassurance
and checking through a known route.

00:02:43.263 --> 00:02:43.963 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
The known route is

00:02:43.963 --> 00:02:47.403 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
stronger because it does not let
the original sender supply all

00:02:47.403 --> 00:02:48.858 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
the evidence for its own claim.

00:02:50.443 --> 00:02:53.623
Imagine a document labeled read-only.

00:02:53.623 --> 00:02:57.603
That may prevent ordinary editing,
but the content can still be readable.

00:02:58.843 --> 00:03:01.019
Encryption addresses a different question:

00:03:01.303 --> 00:03:06.183
can someone understand the data without
the required key or authorized access?

00:03:06.903 --> 00:03:10.530
A password can protect different
actions depending on the application,

00:03:11.123 --> 00:03:15.043
so do not assume every password
prompt means the file is encrypted.

00:03:15.683 --> 00:03:17.911
Inspect what the feature
actually promises.

00:03:18.723 --> 00:03:23.063
In our diagram, a readable file becomes
protected data that requires a key.

00:03:23.623 --> 00:03:24.643
That does not remove

00:03:24.643 --> 00:03:28.283
the need to manage who can access
an unlocked device or account.

00:03:29.183 --> 00:03:32.763
Pause and explain which protection
fits preventing accidental edits,

00:03:32.763 --> 00:03:35.903
and which addresses
readable data being exposed.

00:03:37.992 --> 00:03:39.752
A USB drive found in a parking

00:03:39.752 --> 00:03:44.072
lot might look like an ordinary storage
device. You cannot determine that it

00:03:44.072 --> 00:03:46.974
is safe by its appearance or
by the label written on it.

00:03:47.312 --> 00:03:50.214
Do not plug it into your
computer to discover its owner.

00:03:50.652 --> 00:03:54.175
Give it to authorized staff and
follow the organization's procedure.

00:03:54.792 --> 00:03:56.292
The same general habit applies

00:03:56.292 --> 00:03:59.412
to unfamiliar attachments or
unexpected software prompts:

00:04:00.112 --> 00:04:03.452
stop and verify the purpose
before opening or installing.

00:04:04.092 --> 00:04:06.692
This is not about avoiding
every useful tool.

00:04:06.692 --> 00:04:07.632
It is about

00:04:07.632 --> 00:04:09.052
making the source and task

00:04:09.052 --> 00:04:13.092
clear before granting a device
or file a path into your system.

00:04:13.712 --> 00:04:17.339
Use only the instructor-approved
fictional examples for this exercise.

00:04:19.295 --> 00:04:21.834 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
A video meeting may need
a camera and microphone,

00:04:22.175 --> 00:04:25.495 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
but a simple text page normally
has no reason to use them.

00:04:26.455 --> 00:04:28.165 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
When a permission prompt appears,

00:04:28.315 --> 00:04:32.275 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
identify which app is asking and
what task you are trying to complete.

00:04:33.155 --> 00:04:38.035 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Grant access only when the request fits
that task and you trust the application.

00:04:38.535 --> 00:04:39.875 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Deny an unrelated request,

00:04:40.915 --> 00:04:44.179 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
then review the setting later if
a legitimate feature needs it.

00:04:44.195 --> 00:04:48.288 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
After a meeting, check the app's state
and review permissions when appropriate.

00:04:48.315 --> 00:04:52.855 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
A permission decision is not permanent
approval of everything an app might do.

00:04:53.635 --> 00:04:55.448 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Pause and compare our two examples.

00:04:56.635 --> 00:05:00.015 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Explain why a meeting and a text
page lead to different choices.

00:05:02.107 --> 00:05:03.427
Safety also includes

00:05:03.427 --> 00:05:09.047
the way an online interaction
affects your attention and wellbeing.

00:05:09.047 --> 00:05:12.207
Catfishing uses a false
identity to build trust.

00:05:13.187 --> 00:05:17.547
A friendly conversation does not by
itself verify the person's story.

00:05:18.547 --> 00:05:19.387
Fear of missing

00:05:19.387 --> 00:05:20.227
out can

00:05:20.227 --> 00:05:24.787
encourage repeated checking of alerts even
when you intended to do something else.

00:05:25.687 --> 00:05:30.187
Choose notification settings and
breaks that support your own goals.

00:05:31.347 --> 00:05:35.227
If a post or interaction becomes harmful,
avoid escalating it.

00:05:36.047 --> 00:05:39.427
Use the service's reporting or
blocking tools when appropriate,

00:05:39.427 --> 00:05:43.927
and seek help from a trusted person
or the relevant support channel.

00:05:45.007 --> 00:05:45.947
You do not have

00:05:45.947 --> 00:05:46.787
to solve every

00:05:46.787 --> 00:05:51.707
problem alone or continue a conversation
because someone pressures you to respond.

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:56.010
Here is a new fictional scenario.

00:05:56.870 --> 00:05:57.970
A message claiming

00:05:57.970 --> 00:05:58.730
to represent

00:05:58.730 --> 00:06:00.010
a community service

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:04.470
asks for a private code before
it will confirm an appointment.

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:07.610
Pause the video and state three things:

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:13.290
what evidence you have, what remains
unverified, and what you would do next.

00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:17.030
A strong response identifies
the unusual request,

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:18.670
avoids sharing

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:22.670
the code, and checks the appointment
through a known contact method.

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:28.290
It does not rely only on spelling mistakes
or how professional the message looks.

00:06:28.790 --> 00:06:33.330
Ask a partner to explain a different
scenario using the same process.

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:34.970
If you disagree,

00:06:35.410 --> 00:06:37.490
discuss the evidence behind each choice

00:06:37.490 --> 00:06:40.996
rather than guessing what
answer the instructor wants.

00:06:42.546 --> 00:06:45.655
Before the post-test,
review the skills you can demonstrate:

00:06:46.146 --> 00:06:47.566
adjust a setting and check it,

00:06:47.886 --> 00:06:49.246
justify a source,

00:06:49.866 --> 00:06:55.806
choose file access, test a calculation,
and communicate a clear next step.

00:06:57.326 --> 00:06:59.295
Complete the assessment independently,

00:06:59.786 --> 00:07:03.506
then use its explanations to
identify a useful practice task.

00:07:04.466 --> 00:07:06.726
A classroom score is
one piece of evidence,

00:07:07.046 --> 00:07:11.506
not a judgment about your ability to
learn and not a certification result.

00:07:12.286 --> 00:07:14.514
Keep the saved report
if it helps you plan.

00:07:14.706 --> 00:07:15.586
Choose one skill

00:07:15.586 --> 00:07:16.666
to repeat with less

00:07:16.666 --> 00:07:20.746
help and one situation from your own
routine where it would be useful.

00:07:21.506 --> 00:07:25.986
Confidence grows from being able to
explain and repeat a successful process,

00:07:26.506 --> 00:07:28.886
including knowing when to ask for support.
